# Render the calibration patterns ONCE and keep them on the server.
#
# The client sends two pattern DESCRIPTIONS and nothing else -- no images, no
# colours applied, no polarity worked out. The server renders four PNGs and
# stores them next to the sessions:
#
#   concentric_positive   -> TOP        concentric_negative   -> TOP
#   stripeline_positive   -> N/W/S/E    stripeline_negative   -> N/W/S/E
#
# Why this exists, given ShowPatternSpec already renders on demand:
#
#   * a Pos/Neg shot then costs a lookup and a blit instead of a render. Five
#     panels re-rendered per shot, ten shots a round, five rounds is 250 renders
#     per run; this makes it four, at the moment the operator says the pattern is
#     final.
#   * the pattern that was on the glass is a FILE afterwards. When a round's
#     numbers look wrong, "what exactly was being shown" stops being a question
#     nobody can answer.
#
# The polarity swap is done HERE, not on the client. It is the rule that odd
# layers take the positive colour and even layers the negative one -- arithmetic
# over the operator's design, and therefore the server's job. A client that
# applied it would be a second implementation of it.

# The pattern the operator authored, as the JSON envelope ShowPatternSpec
# documents. Send the layers with whatever colours are in them; they are
# replaced by the pair below.
#
# Empty skips that pattern and leaves any previously prepared copy alone, so the
# two can be updated independently.
string spec_concentric
string spec_stripeline

# The two colours, as [r, g, b], 0..255. positive_rgb goes on the odd layers of
# the POSITIVE pattern and the even layers of the NEGATIVE one; negative_rgb
# does the opposite. That inversion is the whole point of shooting twice.
int32[] positive_rgb
int32[] negative_rgb
---
bool success

# What was written, in the order concentric_positive, concentric_negative,
# stripeline_positive, stripeline_negative. Fewer entries when a spec was empty.
string[] prepared

# The pixel grid each one was rendered at -- the TARGET PANEL's own resolution,
# not a size the client guessed. Same order as `prepared`.
int32[] widths
int32[] heights

# Where they went on the server, so an operator looking at a suspect round can
# find the exact picture that was on the glass.
string directory

string message
